  "title": "Community publishing moved beyond chat",
  "summary": "Pollinations grew into a more connected platform: more kinds of community models to publish, a stateless API for agent workflows, and more ways to use both.\n\nPublishers gained registration and testing for embeddings, synchronous video, and text-to-speech models through My Models, the Account API, and polli. Community text models and endpoint agents can now specify an exact Chat Completions or Responses upstream URL. The wait for community publishing and related endpoint changes fell from 12 hours to 3.\n\nThe stateless `/v1/responses` endpoint gained eligible text models, community endpoints, and managed agents, with streaming where supported. Managed-agent responses now expose ordered assistant messages and executed MCP calls; follow-ups can supply completed tool outputs without running them again. Model discovery identifies supported targets instead of leaving callers to guess.\n\nConnected apps such as Gmail, Slack, GitHub, and Drive became available to agents through Composio MCP using each caller’s connections. A hosted Open WebUI added Pollinations login and opt-in MCP tools, while polli gained setup profiles for Pi, OpenCode, Prime Agent, and OpenClaw. Floret also gained audio tools and catalog-driven model discovery.\n\nNew arrivals covered image generation and editing with FLUX.2 Pro, FLUX.2 Flex, and MAI Image 2.5 Flash; video with MiniMax H3 Max Turbo; and multimodal or long-context text with Inkling, Gemini 3.8 Flash, GPT-6 Astra, and new Qwen models, among others. Wan Pro and Wan 3.0 gained reference-to-video inputs, including end-frame support on Wan 3.0.",
  "pr_count": 127,
